      id: "drinking-fountains",
      type: "Argument",
      title: "Bring Back the Drinking Fountain",
      status: "draft",
      body: [
        {kind: "text", html: "Not long ago, nearly every park, school, and train station had a drinking fountain. Today, many of them are broken, shut off, or <u1>removed. Entirely.</u1> Cities should repair the fountains they have and build new <u2>ones, clean</u2> drinking water should be free and easy to find."},
        {kind: "text", html: "The most obvious benefit is to health. People who can easily get a drink of water are less likely to buy sugary <u3>drinks; which</u3> can lead to tooth decay and other problems. <u4>For instance,</u4> fountains are especially important for people who spend long hours outdoors, such as delivery workers and runners."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Fountains also help the environment. Every bottle of water that a person does not buy is one less plastic bottle that <u5>could end up</u5> in a landfill or an ocean. Some critics argue that fountains are <u6>unsanitary, however,</u6> modern fountains are cleaned regularly, and many have bottle fillers that never touch anyone's mouth."},
        {kind: "text", html: "The cost of a new fountain is small compared with its benefits. A typical outdoor fountain costs a few thousand dollars and lasts for decades. <u7>Some fountains are made of stone, and others are made of steel.</u7> City leaders <u8>whom</u8> care about health, the environment, and fairness should make fountains a priority. After all, water is something <u9>everyone, needs</u9>."}
      ],
      questions: [
        {skill: "structure", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "removed; entirely.", "removed: entirely.", "removed entirely."], answer: 3,
          explanation: "\"Entirely.\" is a fragment: it has no subject or verb. It belongs at the end of the sentence it describes, with no punctuation before it."},
        {skill: "structure", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "ones clean", "ones, because clean", "ones, clean,"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "Both halves are complete sentences, so a comma alone makes a comma splice. \"Because\" fixes it and shows that the second half gives the reason for the first."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "drinks which", "drinks. Which", "drinks, which"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "\"Which can lead to tooth decay\" adds information about sugary drinks. It is not a complete sentence, so it cannot follow a semicolon or a period. A comma sets it off correctly."},
        {skill: "organization", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "Nevertheless,", "As a result,", "In addition,"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "This sentence adds a second health benefit; it is not an example of the sentence before it. \"In addition\" shows that a new point is being added."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "could of ended up", "could ending up", "could ends up"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "After \"could\", the verb takes its plain form: \"could end up\". \"Could of\" is a common mistake for \"could have\"."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "unsanitary however,", "unsanitary; however,", "unsanitary, however"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "Both sides of \"however\" are complete sentences. When \"however\" joins two sentences, it needs a semicolon before it and a comma after it."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 2, stem: "Which choice best supports the writer's claim that the cost of a new fountain is small compared with its benefits?",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "Over those decades, it can fill hundreds of thousands of water bottles at almost no cost.", "Many people prefer their water cold.", "Fountains were common in the early 1900s."], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The claim compares cost with benefits. Only the sentence about filling hundreds of thousands of bottles shows a large benefit for a small cost. The others do not mention cost or benefits."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "who", "which", "whose"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The pronoun is the subject of \"care\", so it should be \"who\", not \"whom\". \"Which\" is for things, not people."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "everyone; needs", "everyone needs", "everyone: needs"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "No punctuation belongs between a subject (\"everyone\") and its verb (\"needs\")."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "Suppose the writer's goal had been to write an essay persuading readers that cities should invest in public drinking fountains. Would this essay accomplish that goal?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it gives health, environmental, and cost reasons for repairing and building fountains.", "Yes, because it describes how fountains are cleaned.", "No, because it focuses mainly on the history of drinking fountains.", "No, because it admits that some critics think fountains are unsanitary."], answer: 0,
          explanation: "The essay states a clear position and supports it with reasons about health, the environment, and cost. Answering a critic's objection makes an argument stronger, not weaker."}
      ]
