      id: "marie-tharp",
      type: "Informative",
      title: "The Woman Who Mapped the Sea Floor",
      status: "draft",
      body: [
        {kind: "text", html: "In the 1940s, few scientists thought anything interesting lay at the bottom of the ocean. Most pictured the sea floor as a flat, muddy plain. Marie Tharp, a young geologist <u1>which</u1> had a talent for drawing, would help prove them wrong."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Tharp joined a research lab at Columbia University in 1948. At the time, women were not allowed on the lab's research <u2>ships so</u2> she stayed on shore. Her job was to turn the depth measurements that male scientists collected at sea <u3>into maps that showed the floor of the sea and what it looked like</u3>."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Working by hand, Tharp plotted thousands of measurements along lines that crossed the Atlantic Ocean. As her drawings took shape, she noticed something <u4>surprising: a</u4> deep valley running down the middle of an underwater mountain range. The valley appeared in every profile she <u5>drew, she</u5> concluded that it ran for thousands of miles."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Tharp believed the valley was a rift, a place where the sea floor was slowly pulling apart. <u6>Her supervisor Bruce Heezen,</u6> at first doubted the idea, because it supported continental drift, a theory that most geologists of the time rejected. But when a colleague mapped where undersea earthquakes <u7>had occurred, it</u7> lined up along the valley Tharp had found. Heezen was convinced, and their work helped lead to the modern theory of plate tectonics."},
        {kind: "text", html: "In 1977, Tharp and Heezen published a painted map of the entire ocean floor. <b8/> The map showed ridges, valleys, and mountains under the sea as clearly as a map of dry land. Today, it is considered one of the most important scientific images of the twentieth century, <u9>and the ocean covers most of the planet</u9>."}
      ],
      questions: [
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "whose", "whom", "who"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "The pronoun refers to a person and is the subject of \"had\", so \"who\" is correct. \"Which\" refers to things."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "ships, so", "ships, so,", "ships so;"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "Two complete sentences joined by \"so\" need a comma before \"so\" and nothing after it."},
        {skill: "style", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "into maps, which were maps of the sea floor", "into maps of the sea floor", "into maps of the floor of the sea and its shape and look"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "A map of the sea floor already shows what the floor looks like, so the extra words repeat the idea. The shortest choice says it clearly."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "surprising; a", "surprising, a,", "surprising a"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "A colon can follow a complete sentence to introduce an explanation of it. Here the words after the colon explain what the surprising thing was. A semicolon would need a complete sentence after it."},
        {skill: "structure", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "drew she", "drew, she,", "drew, so she"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "Both halves are complete sentences, so they cannot be joined by a comma alone. \"So\" joins them and shows that her conclusion followed from what she saw."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 3, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "Her supervisor, Bruce Heezen,", "Her supervisor, Bruce Heezen", "Her, supervisor Bruce Heezen,"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "Tharp had one supervisor, so his name is extra information that renames \"her supervisor\". Extra information like this is set off by a pair of commas, one before it and one after."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "had occurred, they", "occurring, it", "had occurred, this"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The pronoun stands for \"earthquakes\", which is plural, so it must be \"they\"."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 2, stem: "At this point, the writer is considering adding the following sentence: \"It was based on decades of depth measurements, and an artist helped turn Tharp's drawings into a detailed painting.\" Should the writer make this addition here?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it shows that Tharp did none of the work on the map.", "Yes, because it explains how the map was made and leads into the description that follows.", "No, because it contradicts the earlier statement that Tharp drew by hand.", "No, because it repeats the sentence that comes before it."], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The previous sentence says a painted map was published, and the next one describes what it showed. A sentence about how it was made fits between them and adds new information."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "Which choice best concludes the essay by connecting Tharp's early experience to her lasting importance?",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "and maps are still made today", "and Tharp, who was once kept off research ships, is now honored as a pioneer of ocean science", "and Columbia University is in New York City"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "The essay opens with Tharp kept on shore. Mentioning that and her later honors ties her early experience to her importance. The other endings add unrelated facts."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "Suppose the writer's goal had been to describe how scientists collect depth measurements at sea. Would this essay accomplish that goal?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it explains that male scientists collected the measurements.", "Yes, because it describes the research ships in detail.", "No, because it mentions only the Pacific Ocean.", "No, because it focuses on how Tharp used the measurements to map the sea floor, not on how they were collected."], answer: 3,
          explanation: "The essay says only that measurements were collected at sea. It is about what Tharp did with them and what she discovered."}
      ]
