    {
      id: "cooking-class",
      type: "Argument",
      title: "Cooking Belongs in High School",
      status: "draft",
      body: [
        {kind: "text", html: "Most high school students can solve an equation for <em>x</em>, yet many cannot cook <u1>a simple meal for themselves, by themselves, on their own</u1>. Every high school should offer a basic cooking class, and every student should be required to take <u2>them</u2>."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Cooking is a life skill, like managing money or doing laundry. A student who can cook <u3>saves money, eats</u3> healthier food, and is less likely to depend on fast food after graduation. People who cook at home <u4>tends</u4> to eat more vegetables than people who rarely cook."},
        {kind: "text", html: "A cooking class also teaches more than recipes. Students use fractions when they double a <u5>recipe, they</u5> also learn to read directions closely and to work in teams. <u6>On the other hand,</u6> a good cooking class connects to math, reading, and science all at once."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Some people argue that cooking should be taught at home, not at school. <b7/> However, not every family has the time or money to teach it, and a school class gives every student the same chance to learn. The equipment does not have to be <u8>expensive, a</u8> few hot plates, pots, and knives are enough to start."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Schools prepare students for college and careers. They should also prepare students to <u9>chow down</u9>."}
      ],
      questions: [
        {skill: "style", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "on their own a simple meal, by themselves", "a simple meal for themselves, on their own", "a simple meal on their own"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "\"For themselves\", \"by themselves\" and \"on their own\" all say the same thing. Saying it once is clearer."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "those", "it", "they"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "The pronoun stands for \"a basic cooking class\", which is singular, so \"it\" is correct."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "saves money eats", "saves money; eats", "saves, money, eats"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "The sentence lists three things the student does, separated by commas. A semicolon does not separate simple list items, and no comma belongs between \"saves\" and \"money\"."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "is tending", "has tended", "tend"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "The subject is \"people\", not \"home\". \"People\" is plural, so the verb is \"tend\"."},
        {skill: "structure", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "recipe. They", "recipe they", "recipe, and, they"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "Both halves are complete sentences. A period separates them. A comma alone makes a comma splice, and no punctuation makes a run-on."},
        {skill: "organization", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "Even so,", "In this way,", "Instead,"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "This sentence sums up the examples before it (fractions, reading directions, teamwork), so \"In this way\" fits. \"On the other hand\" and \"Even so\" signal a contrast that is not there."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 2, stem: "At this point, the writer wants to acknowledge the opposing view fairly before answering it. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?",
          choices: ["It is true that many families pass down recipes and cooking skills.", "Those people are wrong and should not be listened to.", "Homes have kitchens, and so do some schools.", "Cooking shows are popular on television."], answer: 0,
          explanation: "Admitting that many families do teach cooking treats the other side fairly, and the next sentence (\"However, not every family...\") answers it. Calling the other side wrong is not fair acknowledgment."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "expensive: a", "expensive a", "expensive, and a,"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The second part explains why the equipment need not be expensive. A colon after a complete sentence introduces that kind of explanation. A comma alone would make a comma splice."},
        {skill: "style", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "stuff their faces", "grab some grub", "feed themselves well"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "The essay is written in a serious, persuasive tone. \"Feed themselves well\" matches that tone and the essay's point about healthy eating. The other choices are slang."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "The writer is considering adding the following sentence to the end of the essay: \"A required cooking class would give every graduate a skill they will use every day for the rest of their lives.\" Should the writer make this addition?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it introduces a new argument about school lunches.", "Yes, because it restates the essay's argument in a strong final sentence.", "No, because it contradicts the claim that cooking is a life skill.", "No, because the essay argues that cooking should be taught at home."], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The sentence restates the essay's main claim and its central reason, which makes a strong conclusion. It does not add a new topic or contradict anything."}
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "monarch-relay",
      type: "Informative",
      title: "The Monarch Relay",
      status: "draft",
      body: [
        {kind: "text", html: "Every fall, millions of monarch butterflies leave the northern United States and southern Canada and fly as far as 3,000 miles to the mountains of central Mexico. <u1>Remarkably no</u1> single butterfly makes the round trip."},
        {kind: "text", html: "In spring and summer, an adult monarch lives only two to six weeks. During that time, a female <u2>lay</u2> her eggs on milkweed, the only plant monarch caterpillars can eat. Three or four generations <u3>is</u3> born this way each summer, and each one spreads a little farther north."},
        {kind: "text", html: "The last generation of the summer is different. These butterflies do not lay eggs right away, and they can live for up to eight months. <u4>Its</u4> job is to fly south and survive the winter. Scientists believe the butterflies find their way by using the position of the sun and <u5>a kind of internal clock, which acts as a clock inside their bodies</u5>."},
        {kind: "text", html: "<u6>In contrast, once</u6> they reach Mexico, the monarchs gather in fir forests high in the mountains, where the cool air slows them down and helps them save energy. <u7>They cluster so thickly on the trees, that</u7> branches sometimes bend under their weight."},
        {kind: "text", html: "When spring arrives, the butterflies head north, lay eggs, and <u8>die, and the</u8> relay begins again. <b9/>"}
      ],
      questions: [
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "Remarkably: no", "Remarkably; no", "Remarkably, no"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "\"Remarkably\" introduces the sentence and comments on all of it, so a comma follows it. A semicolon or colon would need a complete sentence before it."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "laid", "lays", "laying"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "The subject \"a female\" is singular, and the passage describes what monarchs do in the present tense, so \"lays\" is right."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "are", "has been", "was"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The subject is \"generations\", which is plural, so the verb must be plural: \"are\"."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "There", "They're", "Their"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "The pronoun stands for \"these butterflies\", which is plural, and shows ownership of \"job\". \"Their\" is the plural possessive. \"There\" is a place and \"they're\" means \"they are\"."},
        {skill: "style", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "a kind of internal clock", "a clock that is internal and inside them", "an internal clock inside their bodies"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "\"Internal\" already means inside their bodies, so the extra words repeat it. The shortest choice says it once."},
        {skill: "organization", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "However, once", "Once", "For instance, once"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "This paragraph simply continues the journey to its end in Mexico. Nothing in it contrasts with or gives an example of the paragraph before, so no transition word is needed."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "They cluster, so thickly on the trees, that", "They cluster so thickly on the trees that", "They cluster so thickly, on the trees that"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "In a \"so ... that\" sentence, no comma comes before \"that\". The two parts are one connected idea."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "die and, the", "die; and, the", "die, the"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "Two complete sentences joined by \"and\" take a comma before \"and\". Leaving out \"and\" would make a comma splice."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "The writer is considering adding the following sentence here: \"In this way, the trip south is made by butterflies whose great-grandparents left Mexico the spring before.\" Should the writer make this addition?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it explains how cool air helps monarchs save energy.", "Yes, because it sums up the relay by showing how the generations are connected.", "No, because it contradicts the claim that no butterfly makes the round trip.", "No, because it shifts the focus to the history of Mexico."], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The essay explains that the trip takes several generations. This sentence sums that up and ties back to the first paragraph's point that no single butterfly makes the round trip."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "Suppose the writer's goal had been to explain how the monarch migration depends on more than one generation of butterflies. Would this essay accomplish that goal?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it explains that monarchs are the only insects that migrate.", "Yes, because it describes how short-lived summer generations and one long-lived fall generation together complete the trip.", "No, because it focuses on what monarch caterpillars eat.", "No, because it describes only the butterflies that spend the winter in Mexico."], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The essay describes the summer generations, the long-lived fall generation, and the return north. That shows the trip depends on several generations. It never claims monarchs are the only insects that migrate."}
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "power-outage",
      type: "Narrative",
      title: "When the Lights Went Out",
      status: "draft",
      body: [
        {kind: "text", html: "The storm knocked out our power at seven o'clock on a Tuesday night, right in the middle of <u1>dinner. My</u1> little brother shrieked, and our dog hid under the table. My father found a flashlight in the kitchen drawer, but <u2>their</u2> batteries were dead."},
        {kind: "text", html: "For a while, we sat in the dark and listened to the rain. Then my mother <u3>had an idea, she</u3> took a box of candles from the closet and lit them one by one. The kitchen <u4>glowed with a soft, warm, glowing light</u4>."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Without the television or our phones, we did not know what to do at first. My brother suggested a card game, and soon all four of us <u5>was</u5> laughing over a game of crazy eights. My <u6>father, who usually worked late</u6> told stories about storms he remembered from his childhood."},
        {kind: "text", html: "The power came back at eleven. The lights snapped on, the refrigerator hummed, and the television blared an advertisement. <u7>Nevertheless,</u7> nobody got up to blow out the candles. We blew them out only when my mother reminded us that it was <u8>passed</u8> midnight. <b9/>"}
      ],
      questions: [
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "dinner, my", "dinner my", "dinner; and my"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "\"The storm knocked out our power...\" and \"My little brother shrieked...\" are separate complete sentences, so a period between them is correct."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "they're", "it's", "its"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "The pronoun stands for \"a flashlight\", which is singular, and shows ownership of the batteries. \"Its\" is the singular possessive. \"It's\" means \"it is\"."},
        {skill: "structure", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "had an idea she", "had an idea: she", "had an idea, and, she"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "Both halves are complete sentences, and the second explains the first. A colon joins them correctly. A comma alone makes a comma splice."},
        {skill: "style", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "glowed softly", "glowed with a glow that was soft and warm", "softly glowed with soft light"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "\"Glowed\" and \"glowing light\" repeat the same idea. \"Glowed softly\" says it in two words."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "is", "were", "has been"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "The subject \"all four of us\" is plural, and the story is in the past tense, so \"were\" is correct."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "father, who usually worked late,", "father who usually worked late,", "father, who usually worked, late"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "\"Who usually worked late\" is extra information about the father, so it needs a comma on both sides. The original is missing the second comma."},
        {skill: "organization", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "Therefore,", "For example,", "Similarly,"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "With the power back, you would expect someone to blow out the candles, but nobody does. That is a contrast, which \"Nevertheless\" signals."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "past", "pass", "passing"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "\"Past\" means after a time, as in \"past midnight\". \"Passed\" is the past tense of the verb \"pass\"."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 2, stem: "The writer wants to end the essay with a sentence that shows how the family felt about the evening. Which choice best accomplishes this goal?",
          choices: ["The storm moved east during the night.", "Candles come in many sizes and colors.", "Our neighbors had a generator.", "None of us had wanted the evening to end."], answer: 3,
          explanation: "Only this choice tells how the family felt. It also explains why nobody blew out the candles when the power came back."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "Suppose the writer's goal had been to write a narrative about how an unexpected event brought a family closer together. Would this essay accomplish that goal?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it shows the family talking and playing together during the outage and not wanting it to end.", "Yes, because it explains how to keep a flashlight working.", "No, because it mainly explains how storms cause power outages.", "No, because the family argues during the outage."], answer: 0,
          explanation: "The outage was unexpected, and without screens the family plays cards and tells stories. They even keep the candles burning after the power returns, which shows the evening brought them together."}
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "night-skies",
      type: "Argument",
      title: "Turn Down the Lights",
      status: "draft",
      body: [
        {kind: "text", html: "For most of human history, people could look up on a clear night and see thousands of stars. Today, most Americans live where the glow of outdoor lights hides all but the brightest stars. That loss is not <u1>permanent, towns</u1> can bring back dark skies with a few simple changes."},
        {kind: "text", html: "The first change is to aim lights downward. Many streetlights and parking lot lights send much of <u2>its</u2> light sideways and up into the sky, where it helps no one. <u3>Shielded fixtures which cover the top of the bulb</u3> direct the light onto the ground, where people need it."},
        {kind: "text", html: "The second change is to use warmer, dimmer lights late at night. Bright blue-white light scatters more in the air than <u4>yellow, orange</u4> light does, so it brightens more of the sky. It also confuses birds that migrate at night and <u5>disturbs</u5> newly hatched sea turtles, which crawl toward the brightest light they see."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Some residents worry that darker streets will be less safe. <u6>However,</u6> well-aimed lights can make it easier to see, because they cause less glare. Glare from a bright, unshielded light can <u7>momentarily blind a driver for a brief moment</u7>, which makes it harder to see a person crossing the street."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Turning down the lights saves energy, protects wildlife, and gives everyone a chance to see the stars. <b8/> It is time for our town to turn down the <u9>lights and look</u9> up."}
      ],
      questions: [
        {skill: "structure", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "permanent, and, towns", "permanent towns", "permanent; towns"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "\"That loss is not permanent\" and \"towns can bring back dark skies\" are both complete sentences. A semicolon can join them; a comma alone cannot."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "it's", "their", "there"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "The pronoun stands for \"streetlights and parking lot lights\", which is plural, so it must be \"their\"."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "Shielded fixtures, which cover the top of the bulb,", "Shielded fixtures, which cover the top of the bulb", "Shielded, fixtures which cover the top of the bulb,"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "\"Which cover the top of the bulb\" explains what shielded fixtures are. Extra information like this is set off by a pair of commas, one before and one after."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "yellow or orange", "yellow, and, orange", "yellow; orange"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The sentence compares blue-white light with one other kind: yellow or orange light. \"Or\" joins the two colors; a comma alone does not."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "disturbing", "has disturbed", "disturb"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "The subject \"it\" (the blue-white light) does two things: it \"confuses\" birds and \"disturbs\" sea turtles. Both verbs match the singular subject and each other."},
        {skill: "organization", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "Therefore,", "For instance,", "Similarly,"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "The writer answers the worry about safety with an opposite point. \"However\" shows that contrast."},
        {skill: "style", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "momentarily blind a driver briefly", "blind a driver for a moment", "blind, for a brief moment, a driver momentarily"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "\"Momentarily\" and \"for a brief moment\" mean the same thing. \"For a moment\" says it once."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 2, stem: "At this point, the writer is considering adding the following sentence: \"Some stars are many times larger than the sun.\" Should the writer make this addition here?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it explains why stars are worth seeing.", "Yes, because it supports the claim that dimmer lights save energy.", "No, because it is a fact about stars that does not support the argument for changing the town's lights.", "No, because it contradicts the claim that light hides the stars."], answer: 2,
          explanation: "The conclusion sums up the reasons to change the town's lights. The size of stars is interesting but has nothing to do with that argument."},
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "lights, and, look", "lights; and look", "lights and, look"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "\"Turn down the lights and look up\" is one subject doing two things, so no comma belongs between them."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "Suppose the writer's goal had been to persuade the town to change its outdoor lighting. Would this essay accomplish that goal?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it describes changes the town could make and gives reasons for them, including safety, wildlife, and energy.", "Yes, because it lists the stars people can see from town.", "No, because it focuses on the history of astronomy.", "No, because it argues that lights should be brighter."], answer: 0,
          explanation: "The essay proposes two specific changes, supports them with reasons, and answers the safety worry. It ends by calling on the town to act."}
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "erie-canal",
      type: "Informative",
      title: "The Ditch That Changed a Nation",
      status: "draft",
      body: [
        {kind: "text", html: "In 1817, New York began digging a canal across the state, from Albany on the Hudson River to Buffalo on Lake Erie. Many people thought the project was foolish. <u1>Critics called it \"Clinton's Ditch,\" after</u1> Governor DeWitt Clinton, who had pushed for it."},
        {kind: "text", html: "The canal was about 363 miles long, and most of it was dug by hand. Workers used shovels, picks, and <u2>horses, they</u2> also invented new tools, such as a machine for pulling up tree stumps. Because the land <u3>rise</u3> more than 500 feet between the Hudson River and Lake Erie, the builders needed locks to raise and lower the boats."},
        {kind: "text", html: "The canal opened in 1825, and the results were dramatic. Before the canal, shipping a ton of goods from Buffalo to New York City cost about $100. <u4>Afterward, it</u4> cost about $10. Farmers in the Midwest could now sell <u5>there</u5> wheat in eastern cities, and factories in the East could ship goods west."},
        {kind: "text", html: "New York City <u6>grew quickly, it</u6> soon became the busiest port in the country. Towns along the route, such as Syracuse and Rochester, <u7>grown</u7> into cities."},
        {kind: "text", html: "Within a few decades, railroads had begun to replace the canal for many kinds of shipping. <b8/> The canal, however, had already changed the country. <u9>It had been dug mostly by hand.</u9>"}
      ],
      questions: [
        {skill: "punct", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "Critics called it \"Clinton's Ditch\" after,", "Critics, called it \"Clinton's Ditch,\" after", "Critics called it, \"Clinton's Ditch,\" after"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "A comma goes inside the closing quotation mark, before the phrase that explains the name. No comma belongs between \"Critics\" and its verb, or between \"called it\" and the name."},
        {skill: "structure", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "horses, they,", "horses they", "horses. They"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "\"Workers used shovels, picks, and horses\" and \"they also invented new tools\" are complete sentences. A period separates them; a comma alone makes a comma splice."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "rises", "rising", "have risen"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The subject \"the land\" is singular, so the verb must be singular: \"rises\"."},
        {skill: "organization", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "For example, it", "Similarly, it", "Instead, it"], answer: 0,
          explanation: "The sentence compares the cost before the canal with the cost after it opened. \"Afterward\" marks that change in time."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 1, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "they're", "their", "its"], answer: 2,
          explanation: "The wheat belongs to the farmers, so the plural possessive \"their\" is correct. \"There\" is a place and \"they're\" means \"they are\"."},
        {skill: "structure", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "grew quickly and soon", "grew quickly it soon", "grew, quickly, it soon"], answer: 1,
          explanation: "Joining two complete sentences with a comma makes a comma splice. Using one subject with two verbs (\"grew\" and \"became\") fixes it."},
        {skill: "grammar", difficulty: 2, stem: "",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "has grown", "growed", "grew"], answer: 3,
          explanation: "The passage is in the past tense, and \"grown\" cannot be used without a helping verb. \"Grew\" is the simple past. \"Has grown\" does not match the plural subject or the time."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 2, stem: "At this point, the writer is considering adding the following sentence: \"Trains were faster than canal boats and could run all year, even when the canal froze in winter.\" Should the writer make this addition here?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it shows that the canal was a failure.", "Yes, because it explains why railroads began to replace the canal.", "No, because it contradicts the claim that the canal changed the country.", "No, because it describes winter weather instead of shipping."], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The previous sentence says railroads replaced the canal for many kinds of shipping. This sentence gives the reason, so it adds useful information in the right place."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "Which choice best concludes the essay by summing up the canal's lasting effects?",
          choices: ["NO CHANGE", "It had cost about $7 million.", "It had opened the Midwest to settlers and made New York City the nation's center of trade.", "It ran through many small towns."], answer: 2,
          explanation: "The sentence before it says the canal had changed the country. Only this choice says how, and it sums up the effects described in the essay."},
        {skill: "development", difficulty: 3, stem: "Suppose the writer's goal had been to explain how the Erie Canal affected the growth of the United States. Would this essay accomplish that goal?",
          choices: ["Yes, because it lists every town along the canal.", "Yes, because it describes how the canal lowered shipping costs and helped cities and the Midwest grow.", "No, because it focuses on how locks work.", "No, because it describes only the canal's construction."], answer: 1,
          explanation: "The essay explains the drop in shipping costs, trade between the Midwest and the East, and the growth of New York City and canal towns. Those are the canal's effects on the country's growth."}
      ]
    }
